INT. WAX MUSEUM - AFTER HOURS

Rows of figures in fading finery. A single lantern. Dust on the Duke of Wellington's boots.

INSPECTOR CALLOWAY (50s, rumpled overcoat, notebook) stands beside a plinth. MADAME ZELENKA (60s, turban, jeweled fingers) sits stiffly on a chaise. PERCIVAL (30s, curator's smock) wrings a handkerchief. LADY MARGUERITE (40s, widow's veil) holds a fan. TOMMASO (20s, night guard's cap) fidgets by the door.

CALLOWAY
Let us review. The Duke was found at midnight with his head missing.

PERCIVAL
It is the wax, Inspector. Wax is delicate.

CALLOWAY
It is also not usually missing.

MARGUERITE
I was admiring the Duke at eleven. He was whole.

ZELENKA
I felt a presence. A cold one. It lingered.

TOMMASO
I locked the doors at ten. No one came in.

CALLOWAY
Then no one went out. Which means the head is here.

PERCIVAL
You can't think one of us...

CALLOWAY
I think a head cannot walk. Madame Zelenka, your turban looks heavy.

ZELENKA
It is Persian silk, Inspector.

CALLOWAY
It is lumpy.

She hesitates. Unwinds the cloth. Inside, nothing but hair. She smiles thinly.

MARGUERITE
Inspector, my fan.

CALLOWAY
Lady Marguerite, your veil has stopped moving.

She stiffens. Tommaso drops his cap. Percival laughs, high and tinny.

PERCIVAL
It's under the chaise, I'm afraid. I only meant to repair the nose.
